<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Crystal Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1851">1851</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Crystal Palace was a building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cast-iron">cast iron</a> and glass designed by Joseph Paxton to house the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-exhibition">Great Exhibition</a> in Hyde Park, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in 1851. After the exhibition it was rebuilt in enlarged form at Sydenham in south London, where it served for exhibitions and entertainment until it was destroyed by fire in 1936.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-crystal-palace.jpg" alt="The Crystal Palace" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philip Henry Delamotte (1821–1889) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crystal_Palace_General_view_from_Water_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The commission in charge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-exhibition">Great Exhibition</a> was established in January 1850, and the project was to be funded by public subscription. An executive building committee that included Isambard Kingdom Brunel and Robert Stephenson, chaired by William Cubitt, invited designs on 15 March 1850. The building had to be temporary, simple, cheap and quick to build, since the exhibition was already scheduled to open on 1 May 1851. The name may come from a piece by Douglas Jerrold in the satirical magazine Punch in July 1850, which spoke of a palace of very crystal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Joseph Paxton's modular design was scaled around the size of the glass panes made by Chance Brothers of Smethwick, the largest then available, at 10 inches by 49 inches. Nearly the whole surface could therefore be glazed with identical panes, which cut cost and building time. More than 1,000 iron columns carried 2,224 trellis girders. Fox, Henderson and Co took possession of the site in July 1850, and more than 5,000 navvies worked on it. It was completed in thirty-nine weeks, with 293,000 panes of glass.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-exhibition">Great Exhibition</a> ran from 1 May to 15 October 1851, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> opened it. Its objects ranged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koh-i-noor">Koh-i-Noor</a> diamond to a hydraulic press and a fire engine. Prices were reduced so that working people could attend from 26 May. Over six million admissions were counted, and the surplus of £186,000 helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoria-and-albert-museum">Victoria and Albert Museum</a>, the Science Museum and the Natural History Museum in South Kensington.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>A consortium of eight businessmen moved the building to Penge Place, at the top of Sydenham Hill, and rebuilt it from 1852 in a quite different, enlarged form. It stood there from June 1854. Its courts told the history of art, and the park held models of dinosaurs made by Benjamin Waterhouse Hawkins. The site also hosted the FA Cup Final from 1895 to 1914. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> more than 125,000 men were trained there for the navy. The move cost far more than the original building, and the company never repaid its debt.</p><p>On the evening of 30 November 1936 an explosion in the women's cloakroom started a fire, and the Penge fire brigade was called. Eighty-nine fire engines and over 400 firemen attended, but within hours the Palace was destroyed. The fire spread quickly in high winds, helped by dry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> flooring and a large quantity of flammable material, and its glow was visible across eight counties.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Crystal Palace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
